# Everything sign-in shows, taken as one contiguous block so the copy reads as
# a group in this file. Written for a television: short sentences, no links, no
# provider jargon, and no instruction that needs a keyboard.
#
# 30044 and 30046 onward are one sentence per outcome in
# resources/lib/auth/errors.py. That module returns a symbolic outcome and a
# bare failure code and holds no words at all, so these are the only place the
# sentences exist and the two cannot drift apart. The pairing:
#
#   30044 unmapped                       30052 mfa_required
#   30046 public_client_flows_off        30053 mfa_enrolment_required
#   30047 consent_required               30054 password_expired
#   30048 admin_consent_required         30055 application_disabled
#   30049 not_assigned_to_application    30056 application_not_in_tenant
#   30050 blocked_by_conditional_access  30057 device_code_rejected
#   30051 blocked_by_security_defaults   30058 device_code_expired
#
# 30045 is appended to any outcome the table marks administrator-must-act, and
# to nothing else. It is the only place the escape hatch is named, which is
# what makes the tenant-block message and the custom identifier setting one
# feature rather than two.

